						<a href="/forum/t-13873471/perfectly-parallel-green-light-wanted">Perfectly parallel (green light wanted)</a>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4827191">
									

<p><strong>Generally, I like it.</strong></p>
<p>Of course I am going to critique a bit but keep in mind I like this idea and I can see potential in it.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">&nbsp;The&nbsp;Critique</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">unga&nbsp;bunga&nbsp;im&nbsp;a&nbsp;collapsible</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p><strong>The Good</strong><br />
Now, I personally dislike cognitohazard, as most new writers usually use it as an excuse for, &quot;SCP-XXXX Makes you kill your family if you look at it. The poles are also a cognitohazard, it makes people around it become a cult, yet it does this over time, and that's the way it's done right. The poles being a cognitohazard is something that could tie into the SCP agent in the central narrative, as you could add points where they consider joining the poles, (If they are going to be around it). Honestly, I haven't searched around enough to find an SCP that makes people become parallel with poles and starve themselves to, so I liked the originality.</p>
<p><strong>The Bad</strong><br />
These poles are must to have a certain range, or it could be considered a world ender for humanity, and because of that certain range, the SCP Foundation could just find what the range is and build a facility outside the range, no robots required. Besides if there was no range to the poles, and the pole started to attract everyone, nations could just destroy the facility around the pole and become cultist.</p>
</div>
</div>
