						in discussion <a href="/forum/c-50864/help:drafts-critique">Writing Help / Help: Drafts Critique</a> &raquo;
						<a href="/forum/t-13870124/kamikaze-mosquitoes">Kamikaze mosquitoes</a>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4827181">
									

<p>Asked to review via IRC. Please keep in mind I am an author / reader, not an official critter.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">hide</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>The area in which SCP-XXXX is found in the wild is guarded by a perimeter.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>A perimeter as in what? I think of a chain-link fence when I read this, but that wouldn't be able to contain mosquitoes.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>All personnel by the perimeter must wear hazmat suits or other equipment that fully covers one's person. Also, you reference the area in the article again so it might be useful to give it a designation such as &quot;Research Site-XXXX&quot; or something.</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>All personnel by the perimeter must wear hazmat suits or other equipment that fully covers one's person.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Weird wording here. Try &quot;All personnel assigned to [designated area] must wear provided personal protective equipment at all times.&quot; You could then put a footnote here outlining what that equipment specifically is.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Personnel on neighboring sites from any level that must be terminated, Agents of groups hostile to the Foundation, and D-class may be used for this. Cause of death: Termination by SCP-XXXX.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is an unneeded tidbit of info. I would just change the sentence before this to say &quot;D-Class individuals&quot;.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Outdated Containment Procedures: If personnel survives an attack of SCP-XXXX, wounds need to be tended and an antihistamine cream must be applied to their skin. Medical staff needs to check for infections1 and remove possible foreign objects from the body. Infected personnel needs to be isolated until they have tested negative twice in succession.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The outdated ConProcs are a weird thing to have in the article. Wouldn't they just remove the outdated ones so that there's no confusion?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX refers to a species of mosquito that only exists in the Ratargul Swamp Forest in Bangladesh.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Instead of &quot;that only exists&quot;, try &quot;whose habitat ranges across&quot;.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>After several attacks, SCP-XXXX got taxonomically classified as the Aedes kamikaze.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Change &quot;attacks&quot; to encounters, change &quot;got&quot; to &quot;was&quot;. Remove &quot;the&quot;.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Larvae and pupae of SCP-XXXX mainly eat honeydew, nectar, and other plant juices until they become adults. Adult specimens suck blood from wildlife which includes but is not limited to; several primates, birds, and smaller lizards. Unlike normal mosquitoes, males from SCP-XXXX also suck blood.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This whole piece can be boiled down to &quot;SCP-XXXX display behavior patterns identical to that of non-anomalous specimens, with the exception of male specimens seeking blood as a food source.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Upon smelling blood or observing humans, specimens will become excited and less predictable. This behavior has been described as if they are experiencing heavy withdrawal symptoms from addiction or as if they are going insane. This climaxes in specimens charging towards the individual (often accompanied with a battle cry), losing maneuverability due to their high speed, and crashing into their target or an object resulting in dead on impact. Specimens also show this behavior when they are about to release offspring or reach the end of their lifecycle.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Nothing much here, but you can continue this from the previous statement. For example &quot;Upon detecting living humans or any amount of human blood, however, SCP-XXXX's behavior will deviate greatly from any known species of Culicidae.&quot;</p>
<p>I'm going to generalize for the addendums from here on out:</p>
<p>Alright, so your addendums are obviously where the meat of story is and do a good job of fleshing out SCP-XXXX to be more than just &quot;killer mosquitoes&quot;. My biggest criticism of them is that they are intimidating. Generally, a wall of text will discourage the reader from continuing to read unless you're amazing at keeping someone's attention. I'd take a look back through your addendums and remove / revise any information provided during the logs that the reader can reasonably deduce themselves or that was already stated previously in the description and containment procedures.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Next individuals up for termination by SCP-XXXX: deathbygin and D-3607.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Guess I'd better put on my <a href="https://images-na.ssl-images-amazon.com/images/I/71F00yixtpL._AC_SY879_.jpg" target="_blank">gear</a> ;)</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

